    id: 'w12-vercase',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'Verification cases in SysML v2: subject, objective, verify, verdict',
    paragraphs: [
      'A verification case definition defines a process for verifying whether a subject satisfies one or more requirements. It always returns a verdict of type VerdictKind, an enumeration with the values pass, fail, inconclusive and error. The requirements to be verified are declared in the case’s objective, and the subject of the verification case is automatically bound to the subject of the verified requirements (S10 sl.96). S06 gives the meanings. Pass: the subject has been determined to satisfy the requirements. Fail: it has been determined not to satisfy them. Inconclusive: no determination could be made. Error: an error occurred while the verification was being performed (S06 p.173). The subject, the “unit under test”, may be general in the definition and specific in usages (S06 p.173).',
      'Formally, a verify inside the objective is a requirement verification usage. It is a subrequirement of the objective, so it is a required constraint of the objective, and its subject is bound by default to the objective’s subject, which is bound to the case’s subject (S06 p.176). This is the difference from Week 10. A satisfy asserts, in the design model, that the requirement holds for a design element (S06 p.168). A verify states what a verification case sets out to show about its subject; the verdict it returns is the evidence. A satisfy next to a fail verdict is a contradiction that the team must resolve.',
      'The steps of a verification case are actions that together determine the verdict. S10’s example parameterises the requirement with an in parameter for the measured value and asserts, inside the requirement, that this value equals the subject’s own feature. The last step calls PassIf, a utility function that returns pass or fail depending on whether its argument is true. Its argument is the requirement itself, invoked with the subject and the processed measurement: a check of whether the requirement is satisfied for those values (S10 sl.96–97). S06 describes the typical steps: collect data about the subject; analyse it, for example a mean and standard deviation over several measurements; then evaluate the result against the objective (S06 p.173–174). A usage can restrict the subject to a test configuration, and individuals can model the actual tests (S10 sl.98–99).',
    ],
    citations: [{ reading: 'R75', pages: '96–99' }, { reading: 'R84', pages: '173–176' }, { reading: 'R83', pages: '168' }],
