      'A complete thread is complete by a rule. The explorer counts the PHY-004 variant of thread 1 as complete: STK-005 > … > REQ-SYS-001 > FN-006 > LOG-002 > PHY-004 > INFO-001 > VER-001 > MOP-002 > R-001. LOG-002 is allocated to the operations centre as well as to the chargers, and the rule then picks an information item linked to PHY-004, here INFO-001, passenger information. Every hop exists, but a charge-time requirement now reaches a passenger-information step. And all ten REQ-SYS can pass verification while MOE-001 fails in service. The digital thread would be the authoritative source of truth (R30 p.1394); it follows, by the instructor’s reasoning, that it is only as true as its links, and a weak hop is the first thing a panel will question (RB-9.3).',
      'Inconclusive is a result, not a failure to report one. VER-004 cannot reach pass or fail while TBD-01 is open: correct holds at a placeholder limit show that the logic works, but say nothing about the approved limit. S06 separates the outcomes precisely: inconclusive means no determination could be made, and error means the performance of the verification went wrong (S06 p.173). The DoD Guidebook adds that each design change must be assessed against the qualified baseline and may require parts of the verification to be repeated (R49 p.150). A verdict is therefore tied to a configuration, which Week 13’s impact analysis will use.',
      'The sources do not draw the V&V line in the same place. MITRE verifies against the requirements of the previous phase and validates against the customer’s operational need (R58 p.433). DoD verifies against the specification and baselines but validates against stakeholder performance requirements in the operational environment (R49 p.149–150). SEBoK’s System Verification article says that verification detects faults while validation “provide[s] evidence for the satisfaction of (system and/or stakeholder) requirements” (R23a p.634, p.639), and its System Validation article (extension reading) compares verification with the system design and validation with the system and stakeholder requirements (R23b p.648). So DoD and SEBoK put stakeholder requirements on the validation side; MITRE and the course put them on the verification side and keep validation for the need. These are differences of baseline, not mistakes; merging them produces the confusion SEBoK lists as its first pitfall, the wrong baseline for the activity (R23a p.640). Name the course convention in your V&V table, and say which baseline a cited source uses.',
